+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface (amended)
+
+**Feature**: `002-phone-redaction-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-20
+
+This document amends [`specs/001-logredact-cli/contracts/cli.md`](../../001-logredact-cli/contracts/cli.md),
+which remains in force for everything not restated here. Sections below marked **AMENDED** replace
+the corresponding section of that contract; sections marked **NEW** are additions; everything else
+in the original is **UNCHANGED** and is not repeated.
+
+Everything stated here is asserted by a test in `tests/test_cli.py`, `tests/test_out_dir.py`, or
+`tests/test_phone.py`.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation — AMENDED
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [-h | --help] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH` and CPython 3.11 or newer. No installation step.
+
+```bash
+PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --count --out-dir clean app.log web.log
+```
+
+## Operands — UNCHANGED
+
+Zero or more `FILE` paths, read in the order given; standard input when none is present. A `FILE`
+of `-` is a literal path to a file named `-`. Options and operands may be interleaved in any
+order, and no option is ever consumed as a `FILE` — now including `--out-dir` and its value
+(FR-040, US2.8).
+
+**One addition**: with `--out-dir`, at least one `FILE` is required. See Exit status 2.
+
+## Options — AMENDED
+
+| Option | Effect |
+|--------|--------|
+| `--count` | After all output is written and flushed, write one tally line to stderr (FR-038, FR-024). With `--out-dir`, written after the summary line (FR-046). |
+| `--out-dir DIR` | **NEW.** Write each `FILE`'s redacted lines to a file of the same base name inside `DIR` instead of to stdout (FR-041). `DIR` and any missing parents are created (FR-042). Requires at least one `FILE` (FR-047). Given without a value, it is a usage error (FR-040). |
+| `-h`, `--help` | Write usage and option descriptions — **which must document `--out-dir`** — to stdout and exit 0, without opening any input source, without creating `DIR` even if `--out-dir` was also given, without writing a tally, and without writing anything to stderr (FR-051). |
+
+Any other option is a usage error. There are still no options to change placeholder text or to
+enable/disable a category (spec Assumptions).
+
+## Standard output — AMENDED
+
+Which of two things appears on stdout depends on `--out-dir`, and nothing else does:
+
+| Mode | Content of stdout |
+|---|---|
+| Without `--out-dir` | One concatenated stream of redacted source contents, in source order — unchanged in every respect (FR-004, FR-052) |
+| With `--out-dir`, successful run | Exactly `wrote N files\n` and nothing else (FR-045, US2.2) |
+| With `--out-dir`, failing run | No summary line. Whatever redacted output had been written goes to output files, never here (FR-047 – FR-050, SC-017) |
+| Help run | Usage text and nothing else (FR-051) |
+
+The fidelity guarantees (FR-005, FR-005a, FR-007, FR-007a) move with the redacted bytes: without
+`--out-dir` they describe stdout, with `--out-dir` they describe each output file (FR-044).
+
+### Redactions — AMENDED
+
+| Input | Output | Counted as |
+|-------|--------|------------|
+| Email address | `[EMAIL]` | `emails` |
+| IPv4 address | `[IP]` | `ips` |
+| Luhn-valid 13–19 digit card number | `[CARD]` | `cards` |
+| **`+` followed by 8–15 digits, not followed by a further digit** | **`[PHONE]`** | **`phones`** |
+
+Recognition rules are in [redaction-patterns.md](./redaction-patterns.md). Each character of a
+line belongs to at most one redaction; the earliest-starting match wins, and at the same start
+position the order is email, then IPv4, then card, then phone (FR-035, FR-036). Placeholder text
+already in the input — now including `[PHONE]` — is passed through unchanged and uncounted
+(FR-037).
+
+## Output files — NEW
+
+Present only with `--out-dir`.
+
+| Property | Value | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Location | `DIR/<basename of FILE>` — the final path component only, leading directories discarded | FR-041 |
+| Count | One per `FILE` operand, in operand order | FR-041 |
+| Contents | Byte-for-byte what stdout would have carried for that `FILE` alone without `--out-dir` | FR-044 |
+| Existing file of that name | Replaced in full, never appended to | FR-043 |
+| Two operands sharing a base name | The later operand's output replaces the earlier's; both still count toward `N` | FR-043, FR-045 |
+| Empty input file | Empty output file, which still counts toward `N` | US2.6 |
+| Encoding | UTF-8, `errors="surrogateescape"`, `newline="\n"` — the same configuration as stdout | FR-044 |
+| Writing style | Streamed line by line as the input is read; no temporary file, no rename, no rollback | spec Assumptions, SC-018 |
+
+`DIR` itself is created, with any missing parents, after the command line is accepted and before
+any input is read. An existing `DIR` is used as it is and its unrelated contents are left alone
+(FR-042, US2.4, spec edge cases).
+
+## Standard error — AMENDED
+
+| Condition | Content |
+|-----------|---------|
+| `--count`, successful run | Exactly `emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N\n` (FR-038) |
+| No `--count`, successful run | Empty (FR-025) |
+| Help run | Empty (FR-051) |
+| Unreadable `FILE` | One diagnostic containing that path as spelled on the command line; **no** tally line, even with `--count`, and **no** summary line (FR-028, FR-050) |
+| `--out-dir` with no `FILE` | One diagnostic naming the conflict; no tally (FR-047) |
+| `DIR` unusable | One diagnostic naming `DIR`; no tally, no summary (FR-048) |
+| Output file unusable | One diagnostic naming that output path; no tally, no summary (FR-048) |
+| Output path is its own input `FILE` | One diagnostic naming that file; no tally, no summary (FR-049) |
+| Invalid usage | argparse usage line plus the error (FR-030, FR-040) |
+| Failed write to stdout | One concise diagnostic that output could not be written; no tally (FR-031a) |
+
+Redacted log content is never written here (FR-031). No failure path emits a traceback or other
+unhandled-error text (FR-048, SC-016).
+
+Diagnostic forms. The `logredact: error: ` prefix and the quoted path are stable and asserted; the
+trailing OS message is not asserted verbatim.
+
+```text
+logredact: error: cannot read 'missing.log': [Errno 2] No such file or directory
+logredact: error: failed writing to standard output
+logredact: error: --out-dir requires at least one FILE operand; standard input has no file name
+logredact: error: cannot use output directory 'clean': [Errno 17] File exists: 'clean'
+logredact: error: cannot write output file 'clean/app.log': [Errno 21] Is a directory: 'clean/app.log'
+logredact: error: output file for 'app.log' would overwrite that file: './app.log'
+```
+
+The first two are unchanged from feature 001 (FR-052).
+
+## Exit status — UNCHANGED
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|--------|---------|
+| `0` | Every named source was read to completion and all output was written, flushed, and closed successfully; or `--help` was requested |
+| `2` | Anything else: unreadable source, invalid usage, unusable `DIR`, unusable output file, output path colliding with its input, or output that could not be fully written |
+
+Status `1` is still never produced. A `wrote N files` line appears **only** on a run that exits 0,
+so the summary never implies a set of files that is not there (SC-017).
+
+## Order of operations — NEW
+
+With `--out-dir` the order is itself a requirement (FR-054), because it determines what is on
+disk after a failure:
+
+1. Parse the command line. A help request (FR-051) and usage errors (FR-040, FR-047) are handled
+   here and **leave the filesystem untouched** — no `DIR`, no input opened, no stdin read.
+2. Create `DIR` with its parents and confirm it is usable, **before any input source is opened**
+   (FR-042, FR-048).
+3. Check the computed output path of **every** operand against that operand (FR-049, FR-049a). A
+   collision in any operand — including the third of three — fails the run here, before any output
+   file has been opened and before any input has been read (US4.7c).
+4. For each operand in order: open the input, re-check the collision, open the output file, stream
+   the redacted lines, close the output file.
+5. Write `wrote N files` to stdout and flush it.
+6. Write the tally to stderr if `--count` was given.
+
+A run that fails at step 2 or 3 has created, replaced, or truncated **no** output file. A run that
+fails at step 4 leaves output files already completed for earlier operands on disk, complete and
+correct; the operand being processed may be left partially written. Nothing is deleted or rolled
+back — exit status 2 and the absent summary line are what tell the caller not to trust the set
+(FR-050, FR-054).
+
+Without `--out-dir`, steps 2, 3 and 5 do not happen and step 4 writes to stdout, which is
+feature 001's behaviour unchanged (FR-052).
+
+## Ordering and buffering — AMENDED
+
+1. All redacted output is written: to stdout, or to output files which are each closed as their
+   operand completes.
+2. With `--out-dir`, the summary line is written to stdout.
+3. stdout is flushed. A failure at step 2 or 3 is a failed write: exit 2, no tally (FR-031a).
+4. Only then is the tally written to stderr, if `--count` was given (FR-024, FR-046).
+
+A consumer redirecting the two streams separately therefore still always sees a complete result
+before the summary.
+
+## Worked examples — NEW
+
+All reproduce byte-exactly (SC-010). `$` lines are commands; the rest is output as labelled.
+
+### 1. Phone number in piped input (US1.1)
+
+```bash
+$ printf 'call +14155552671 now\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact
+call [PHONE] now
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+### 2. Output folder with two files (US2.1)
+
+```bash
+$ printf 'user jane@example.com\n' > app.log
+$ printf 'GET / from 10.0.0.7\n'   > web.log
+$ PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log web.log
+wrote 2 files
+$ echo $?
+0
+$ cat clean/app.log
+user [EMAIL]
+$ cat clean/web.log
+GET / from [IP]
+```
+
+### 3. Four-field tally (US3.1)
+
+```bash
+$ printf 'a@b.io +442071838750\n' | PYTHONPATH=src python -m logredact --count
+[EMAIL] [PHONE]                    # stdout
+emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=1    # stderr
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+## Behaviour under a closing reader — UNCHANGED
+
+Unchanged for the stdout path. With `--out-dir` there is almost nothing on stdout to close a pipe
+against, but the summary line is still written through the same helpers, so
+`python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log | true` fails the same way if the write cannot
+complete: one diagnostic, exit 2, no tally, no traceback (FR-031a).
+
+## Internal entry point — UNCHANGED
+
+`logredact.cli.main(argv: list[str]) -> int` remains importable and still returns the exit status
+rather than calling `sys.exit`. Still an implementation convenience, not a supported library API.
