+# Contract: redaction recognition grammar (amended)
+
+**Feature**: `002-phone-redaction-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-20
+
+This document amends
+[`specs/001-logredact-cli/contracts/redaction-patterns.md`](../../001-logredact-cli/contracts/redaction-patterns.md).
+The email, IPv4, and card grammars there are **unchanged** and are not repeated. This adds the
+fourth category and restates the precedence rule, which is the only part of the original grammar
+contract this feature touches.
+
+The fragments below are the literal source of `src/logredact/redact.py`; the module copies them
+verbatim so the two cannot drift.
+
+---
+
+## New category: phone
+
+```python
+_PHONE = r"\+[0-9]{8,15}(?![0-9])"
+```
+
+| Part | Means | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `\+` | A single literal `+`. Nothing before it is inspected. | FR-033, spec Assumptions |
+| `[0-9]{8,15}` | Eight to fifteen digits, inclusive, immediately after the `+`, with no separators, spaces, or other characters among them. | FR-033 |
+| `[0-9]` **not** `\d` | "Digit" is `0`–`9` only. `\d` would additionally match Arabic-Indic, Devanagari, and full-width digits, breaking FR-033a in both directions. | FR-033a |
+| `(?![0-9])` | The candidate must not be immediately followed by a further `0`–`9`. | FR-034 |
+
+There is **no post-validation step**. Unlike `card`, a phone candidate that the pattern matches is
+redacted; `_longest_valid_card` is still reached only for the `card` group.
+
+### Why the lookahead is sufficient for FR-034
+
+A sixteen-digit run is the case worth spelling out, because the obvious expectation is wrong. The
+engine tries fifteen digits, the lookahead sees the sixteenth and fails, so it backtracks to
+fourteen — where the lookahead now sees the fifteenth digit and fails again — and so on down to
+eight. Every length fails, so the whole alternative fails at that start position. There is no
+"first fifteen digits" partial match and no stray trailing digit. The card rule then gets its turn
+one character to the right, exactly as it does today, so `+4111111111111111` is still `+[CARD]`.
+
+### Accepted and rejected forms
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `+14155552671` | `[PHONE]` | 11 digits |
+| `+442071838750` | `[PHONE]` | 12 digits |
+| `+12345678` | `[PHONE]` | 8 digits — the lower bound |
+| `+123456789012345` | `[PHONE]` | 15 digits — the upper bound |
+| `+1234567` | unchanged | 7 digits, too short |
+| `+1234567890123456` | no `[PHONE]` | 16 digits; falls through to the card rule |
+| `+٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨` | unchanged | Arabic-Indic digits are not `0`–`9` |
+| `+12345678٩` | `[PHONE]٩` | `٩` neither extends the candidate nor satisfies the exclusion |
+| `+1 415 555 2671` | unchanged | separators are not accepted; the digit groups are too short for any rule |
+| `14155552671` | unchanged | no `+`; too short for a card candidate |
+| `+` | unchanged | no digits |
+| `++14155552671` | `+[PHONE]` | the second `+` begins the candidate |
+| `+abc12345678` | unchanged | the digits do not immediately follow the `+` |
+| `total=5+12345678` | `total=5[PHONE]` | nothing before the `+` is inspected |
+| `[PHONE]` | unchanged, uncounted | no `+` followed by digits; FR-037 |
+| `+123` *newline* `45678` | unchanged | matching never spans a line boundary |
+
+A byte that is not valid UTF-8 appears in the decoded line as a lone surrogate, which is not in
+`[0-9]`, so it breaks a candidate like any other non-digit and still passes through unchanged
+(FR-005a).
+
+---
+
+## Precedence — AMENDED
+
+```python
+PATTERN = re.compile(
+    rf"(?P<email>{_EMAIL})|(?P<ip>{_IPV4})|(?P<card>{_CARD})|(?P<phone>{_PHONE})"
+)
+```
+
+**The written order of the alternatives is the precedence rule.** `re.search` scans start
+positions left to right and, at each position, tries the alternatives in written order. That gives
+earliest-start-wins with written order as the tiebreak, which is FR-021 as amended by FR-036:
+email, then IPv4, then card, then phone. Reordering the alternatives changes behaviour and nothing
+will warn you.
+
+Every inner group remains non-capturing, so `match.lastgroup` reliably names the category.
+
+### The two cases precedence decides
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `+14155552671@example.com` | one `[EMAIL]` | Both categories start at offset 0 — the existing email local-part class already contains `+` and digits — and email is written first (FR-036). |
+| `+4111111111119` | `[PHONE]`, not `+[CARD]` | The phone candidate starts at the `+`, one character *earlier* than the 13-digit Luhn-valid card candidate, so earliest-start-wins settles it. No tiebreak is involved. |
+
+The second row is the one input shape whose redaction output differs from before this feature
+(spec Assumptions, SC-012). It is a different placeholder on text that was already redacted, never
+a leak.
+
+Phone and card can never start at the same offset — a card candidate starts on a digit, a phone
+candidate on `+` — so phone's position in the order relative to card is observationally neutral.
+It is written last because FR-036 says last.
+
+---
+
+## Interaction with the card rejection loop — UNCHANGED
+
+`redact_line`'s two cursors are unchanged. When a card candidate fails the Luhn checksum, only the
+search cursor advances, by one character (FR-019a). Because that resume never moves *forward* past
+unexamined text and because a card candidate contains no `+`, a phone number lying beyond a
+rejected card candidate is still found:
+
+```text
+1234567890123+12345678   ->   1234567890123[PHONE]
+```
+
+The search cursor still strictly increases on every iteration, so the loop still terminates.
+
+---
+
+## Placeholders — AMENDED
+
+| Category | Placeholder | Tally key |
+|---|---|---|
+| email | `[EMAIL]` | `emails` |
+| ip | `[IP]` | `ips` |
+| card | `[CARD]` | `cards` |
+| **phone** | **`[PHONE]`** | **`phones`** |
+
+Placeholders are appended to the output fragment list and never substituted into the string being
+scanned, so substituted text is never re-examined and a literal `[PHONE]` in the input is passed
+through unchanged and uncounted (FR-037). All four are fixed and non-configurable.
